      "text": "To determine the number of students who did not receive an A in either history or math, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following sets:\n\n- Let \\( H \\) be the set of students who received an A in history.\n- Let \\( M \\) be the set of students who received an A in math.\n\nWe are given the following information:\n- The total number of students is \\( |U| = 30 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in history is \\( |H| = 7 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in math is \\( |M| = 13 \\).\n- The number of students who received an A in both history and math is \\( |H \\cap M| = 4 \\).\n\nWe need to find the number of students who received an A in either history or math or both, which is \\( |H \\cup M| \\). According to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nThis means that 16 students received an A in either history or math or both. To find the number of students who did not receive an A in either history or math, we subtract the number of students who received an A in at least one of the courses from the total number of students:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of students who did not receive an A in either course} = |U| - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
